# Cursor Rules for Flask Game Architecture

This file defines coding standards and architectural patterns for the Flask game codebase. All AI-generated code MUST adhere to these rules to maintain consistency with the existing codebase.

## Project Structure

The project follows a Flask application structure:
- `app.py` - Main Flask application with route handlers
- `utils.py` - Utility functions for database queries (Supabase)
- `templates/` - Jinja2 templates with inheritance hierarchy
- `static/style/` - CSS files (global.css as base + per-page stylesheets)
- `static/js/` - JavaScript files (per-page where needed)
- `static/assets/` - Image assets (pixel art style)

## Template Inheritance Hierarchy

Templates MUST follow this inheritance chain:
1. Base: `layout.html` - Contains HTML structure, head, fonts, global CSS
2. Intermediate: `clues_wrapper.html` - Adds clue modal system (extends layout.html)
3. Specific pages: Extend either `clues_wrapper.html` (for pages with clues) or `layout.html` (for standalone pages)

Template structure:
```
layout.html
├── clues_wrapper.html (extends layout.html)
│   ├── map.html
│   ├── police_station.html
│   ├── bakery.html
│   └── bank.html (intermediate for bank pages)
│       ├── bank_atm.html
│       └── bank_account.html
├── index.html (extends layout.html directly)
└── error.html (extends layout.html directly)
```

## Python/Flask Standards

### Route Handlers
- Use lowercase with hyphens for route paths: `/police-station`, `/bank/accounts`
- Separate GET and POST logic with `if request.method == "GET"` and `if request.method == "POST"`
- Always pass form values back via `prev_values` dictionary in template context to preserve form state
- Use `render_template()` with explicit keyword arguments for data passing
- Handle errors with try/except blocks, fallback to rendering template with previous values

Example pattern:
```python
@app.route("/route-name", methods=["GET", "POST"])
def route_name():
    if request.method == "GET":
        return render_template("template.html", prev_values=None, data=None)
    if request.method == "POST":
        prev_values = {
            "field1": request.form.get("field1"),
            "field2": request.form.get("field2")
        }
        # Validation logic here
        data = utility_function(prev_values)
        return render_template("template.html", prev_values=prev_values, data=data)
```

### Utility Functions (utils.py)
- All database queries MUST use Supabase client created via `create_client(os.getenv("SUPABASE_URL"), os.getenv("SUPABASE_KEY"))`
- Functions should accept a `form` dictionary parameter containing form values
- Build queries conditionally: check if form values exist before adding filters
- Use `.ilike()` for case-insensitive text searches, `.eq()` for exact matches
- Return query results via `.execute().data`
- Use snake_case for function names: `get_police_data()`, `get_security_logs()`, `get_atm_transactions()`
- Helper functions like `validate_values()` and `escape_strings()` should be kept in utils.py

### Error Handling
- Use Flask's `@app.errorhandler(404)` for 404 errors
- Custom errors should render `error.html` template with `message` parameter
- Always include `@app.after_request` hook to disable caching:
```python
@app.after_request
def after_request(response):
    """Ensure responses aren't cached"""
    response.headers["Cache-Control"] = "no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate"
    response.headers["Expires"] = 0
    response.headers["Pragma"] = "no-cache"
    return response
```

## Jinja2 Template Standards

### Template Blocks
- Use `{% extends "parent_template.html" %}` at the top
- Define `{% block title %}` for page titles
- Define `{% block stylesheet %}` for page-specific CSS links using `url_for('static', filename='style/filename.css')`
- Use `{% block content %}` or `{% block body %}` as appropriate
- Use `{% block bank_form %}` or similar named blocks for component inheritance (see bank.html pattern)

### Form Handling
- All form inputs MUST preserve values using `value="{{ prev_values['field'] if prev_values else '' }}"`
- Select dropdowns MUST preserve selected state: `{% if prev_values and prev_values.field == 'value' %} selected="selected" {% endif %}`
- Dynamic labels/placeholders should check `prev_values` state (see police_station.html pattern)
- Forms should use `method="POST"` and `action="/route-path"`

### Data Display
- Check for data existence with `{% if data != None %}` before rendering results
- Use `{% if data|count > 0 %}` to check for empty lists
- Always show "No results found - try again!" message when data is empty
- Results should be in `<ul>` lists or `<table>` structures
- List items MUST have class `pixel-box list-item` for consistent styling

### Clue System Integration
- Clue buttons MUST use `onclick="addToClues('category', 'formatted string')"` pattern
- Format clue strings consistently: `'{{ row.date }} @ {{ row.time }}: {{ row.activity }} by {{ row.license_plate }}'`
- Clue buttons MUST have classes `pixel-box icon-button` and `title="Save clue"` or `title="Add to clues"`
- Clue button text is "+" for adding, "x" for removing (handled in JavaScript)

### Image Tags
- ALL images MUST include `alt` text describing the pixel art
- Use `{{url_for('static', filename='/assets/image.png')}}` for image sources
- Format: `alt="A pixel-art [description]"` or descriptive alt text

### Navigation Links
- "Back to the map" links MUST be wrapped in `<div class="link-container">` with class `pixel-box`
- Link structure: `<a class="pixel-box" href="/map">Back to the map</a>`

## CSS Architecture

### File Organization
- `global.css` - Base styles applied to all pages (fonts, pixel-box class, common utilities)
- Page-specific CSS files: `index.css`, `map.css`, `police-station.css`, `bakery.css`, `bank.css`, `error.css`
- Page-specific CSS extends global.css via template blocks

### Core CSS Classes (in global.css)
- `.pixel-box` - Pixel-art styled borders using pseudo-elements (`:before` and `:after`)
- `.fullscreen-center` - Flexbox centering for full viewport height
- `.page-container` - Main content wrapper (flex column, centered, padding)
- `.link-container` - Navigation link wrapper (centered, padding)
- `.input-group` - Form input wrapper (flex column, aligned left)
- `.list-item` - List item styling (flex column, margin-bottom)
- `.icon-button` - Small icon buttons (width: 1.5rem)
- `.hidden` - Display none utility
- `.spacing` - Content spacing wrapper (width: 80-90%, max-width: 800px, padding)

### Typography
- Font family: `"Jersey 15", sans-serif` on body and buttons
- Font weight: 400 (normal)
- Font style: normal

### Pixel-Box Implementation
The `.pixel-box` class uses pseudo-elements to create a pixel-art border effect:
- Main element has `border-style: solid; border-width: .125em 0`
- `:before` pseudo-element creates vertical borders (position: absolute, z-index: -1)
- `:after` pseudo-element creates outer border effect (z-index: -2)
- Colors: white background with black borders

### Responsive Design
- Breakpoints: 800px, 600px, 400px
- Forms switch from flex to grid at 800px: `grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr`
- Forms become single column at 400px: `grid-template-columns: auto`
- Use `@media screen and (max-width: Xpx)` for screen-based queries
- Use `@media (max-width: Xpx)` for general queries
- Use `@media (hover: hover)` for hover effects (prevents mobile tap issues)

### Common Patterns
- Headers: `h1 { text-align: center; }` in page-specific CSS
- Tables: `border: 1px solid black; border-collapse: collapse;` with `padding: 0.5em` on cells
- Hover effects: `transform: scale(1.05); transition: transform 0.3s;` on interactive elements
- Use `em` units for spacing (not px or rem)

### CSS Nesting
- Use `&` for pseudo-classes and nested selectors
- Example: `.clue-control, .link-container, button { ... &:hover { ... } }`

## JavaScript Standards

### File Organization
- `index.js` - Global clue management system (loaded in clues_wrapper.html)
- Page-specific JS files (e.g., `police_station.js`) - Page-specific interactions

### Clue System Functions (index.js)
- `addEventControllers()` - Sets up clue modal open/close events
- `addToClues(category, clue)` - Adds clue to localStorage, alerts user, rebuilds list
- `removeFromClues(category, clue)` - Removes clue from localStorage, rebuilds list
- `buildClueList()` - Rebuilds clue display from localStorage, includes default starter clue

### localStorage Structure
- Keys are category strings (e.g., "crime scene reports", "interviews", "bakery security logs")
- Values are JSON arrays of clue strings
- Check for duplicates before adding: `if (list.find(c => c === clue)) return alert("clue already saved");`

### Event Handling
- Use `addEventListener()` not inline handlers (except for clue buttons in templates)
- Use `querySelector()` and `querySelectorAll()` for DOM access
- Handle modal backdrop clicks to close modals
- Use `e.stopPropagation()` to prevent event bubbling on modal body

### Naming Conventions
- camelCase for variables and functions: `addEventControllers`, `clueModel`, `closeModelButton`
- Use descriptive variable names: `clueControl`, `modelBody`, `closeModelButton`

### Common Patterns
- Function declarations (not arrow functions for top-level functions)
- Arrow functions for event handlers and callbacks
- Check element existence before adding event listeners
- Use `classList.add()` and `classList.remove()` for toggling classes

## Naming Conventions

### Python
- Functions: `snake_case` - `get_police_data()`, `validate_values()`
- Variables: `snake_case` - `prev_values`, `report_data`, `form`
- Constants: Uppercase with underscores (if used)

### HTML/Classes
- CSS classes: `kebab-case` - `pixel-box`, `link-container`, `input-group`, `page-container`
- IDs: `kebab-case` - `clue-control`, `clue-model`, `close-model`

### JavaScript
- Variables: `camelCase` - `clueControl`, `closeModelButton`, `modelBody`
- Functions: `camelCase` - `addToClues()`, `buildClueList()`

### Routes/URLs
- Use hyphens: `/police-station`, `/bank/accounts` (not underscores)

## Data Flow Pattern

1. User submits form (POST request)
2. Route handler extracts form values into `prev_values` dictionary
3. Route handler calls utility function from `utils.py` with `prev_values`
4. Utility function creates Supabase client and builds query conditionally
5. Query results returned as list of dictionaries
6. Route handler passes `prev_values` and `data` to template
7. Template displays results and preserves form values
8. User can save clues via JavaScript `addToClues()` function
9. Clues stored in localStorage and displayed in modal

## Error Handling Patterns

- Form validation should happen in route handlers before calling utils
- Validation errors should render error template with descriptive message
- Missing values should be handled gracefully (show empty results rather than crash)
- Try/except blocks should fall back to rendering template with previous values
- Use `print("exception")` for debugging (not proper error logging, but matches existing pattern)

## Asset References

- Images: `/static/assets/` directory
- CSS: `/static/style/` directory (use `url_for('static', filename='style/filename.css')`)
- JavaScript: `/static/js/` directory (use relative paths like `../static/js/filename.js` in templates)
- Favicon: `/static/assets/cs50-duck.png`

## Code Style Examples

### Adding a New Route
```python
@app.route("/new-location", methods=["GET", "POST"])
def new_location():
    if request.method == "GET":
        return render_template("new_location.html", prev_values=None, data=None)
    if request.method == "POST":
        prev_values = {
            "field1": request.form.get("field1"),
            "field2": request.form.get("field2")
        }
        data = get_new_data(prev_values)
        return render_template("new_location.html", prev_values=prev_values, data=data)
```

### Adding a New Utility Function
```python
def get_new_data(form):
    supabase = create_client(
        os.getenv("SUPABASE_URL"), 
        os.getenv("SUPABASE_KEY"))
    query = supabase.table("table_name").select()
    if form["field1"]:
        query.eq("column", form["field1"])
    if form["field2"]:
        query.ilike("column", f"%{form['field2']}%")
    return query.execute().data
```

### Adding a New Template (with clues)
```jinja2
{% extends "clues_wrapper.html" %}

{% block title %}
    Page Title
{% endblock %}

{% block stylesheet %}
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ url_for('static',filename='style/page-name.css') }}">
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
    <h1>Page Heading</h1>
    <div class="page-container">
        <form action="/route" method="POST">
            <div class="input-group">
                <label for="field">Label:</label>
                <input id="field" name="field" type="text" value="{{ prev_values['field'] if prev_values else '' }}">
            </div>
            <button class="pixel-box" type="submit">Search</button>
        </form>
        <div class="link-container">
            <a class="pixel-box" href="/map">Back to the map</a>
        </div>
        {% if data != None %}
            <!-- Display results -->
        {% endif %}
    </div>
{% endblock %}
```

## Important Notes

- NEVER use async/await in JavaScript - use callbacks and promises
- NEVER use TypeScript - this is plain JavaScript
- NEVER use CSS preprocessors (SASS/SCSS) - use CSS with nesting support
- NEVER use modern Python features beyond what's in existing code (no type hints, dataclasses, etc.)
- ALWAYS preserve form values when re-rendering after POST
- ALWAYS use Supabase for database queries (no direct SQL)
- ALWAYS include alt text on images
- ALWAYS use `url_for()` for static file references in templates
- ALWAYS maintain the pixel-art aesthetic with `.pixel-box` class